Sculpt and rig the **Aegis**, a colossal multi-gun walking fortress that strides on
six independent legs — each a three-segment thigh/shin/flat-foot chain — whose main
cannon aims forward, whose two side-mounted turrets each traverse to cover only their
own flank, and whose radar vane sweeps on its own, as a rigged 3D mesh in an
88x80x104 volume using only the `sn-anim` tool. You author its walk and weapon
animations as F-curves.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to shape the model and edit the rig is the `sn-anim` binary already on
your `PATH` — a **Surface Nets** meshing tool. It does **not** place cells: it
maintains a **continuous signed-distance field** you shape by **compositing
primitives** (`add-sphere`/`add-box`/`add-ellipsoid`/`add-cylinder`, their
`subtract-` counterparts, a `--blend` radius for smooth joins, plus `mirror`,
`translate`, `copy`, `replace-color`, `clear`), and it extracts the field's surface
with Surface Nets — so the result is **smooth, watertight, and rounded**, with no
sharp edges. Design the fortress to that character: heavy cast/molded armor built
from big blended volumes, not faceted blocks or crisp knife-edges.

It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation at a
time**, and the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part — together with the
per-part mesh it emits and the rig you define are the **authoritative output that is
scored**, not any file you write yourself. Anything produced another way (writing
files directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the
recorded operations, the emitted meshes, and the rig count.

Every operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is composited
**separately** into its own field, meshed on its own into its own
`parts/<part>.mesh.json`, with its own preview and log. The field starts **empty**.
Colors are **opaque** `#rrggbb` — there is no transparency. After each call the
binary re-extracts that part's mesh and re-renders its 3D preview to
`parts/<part>.png`. Blank starting previews, empty per-part logs, and a pre-seeded
`rig.json` holding the required parts, joints, and animation declarations are already
in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
sn-anim add-box --part chassis --x 44 --y 45 --z 52 --width 68 --height 24 --depth 88 --color "#c69a4b" --blend 4
```

Run `sn-anim --help` to list every field operation **and** the rig subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and defining animations and their
keyframes), and `sn-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that
help text is the authoritative contract. **Read `parts/<part>.png` after your
calls** to see that part's meshed surface, **and read the assembled-scene previews
under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`,
`scene/top.png`)** — the whole fortress composed from all your parts, re-rendered
after every operation. The scene views are how you confirm the parts actually fit
together: that the six legs seat under the hull and reach the ground, the main turret
sits centered on the citadel, the cannon meets the turret front, and each side turret
seats out on its sponson. Because each part is composited in the shared volume's
coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the finished fortress; the scene
simply composes them there.

